A||||6||862M4inCA||swapping 2.5 for 2.8||||1||
Z||000000||862M4inCA||03-21-2001||10:43 AM||||Just toying with the idea of a 2.5 to 2.8 swap and was reading some information on the web about someone who's done one. Seems as though it would be easy enough but I'd just like to confirm a couple things. I realize that the fuel pump, radiator (should), ECM, and engine wiring harness needs to be changed. However the page I was reading also mentioned that the dash wiring also needed to be changed. Also, does the accelerator cable need to be changed or will the 2.5 cable work? Any other information would be appreciated since if I found a good deal on a complete 2.8 here in town that's getting pulled for a V8 conversion. I'd like to jump on this opportunity before someone else, and the guy will have it ready in a week or so. Thanks guys||||reg||1
Z||000001||Philphine||03-21-2001||10:48 AM||||it's all trying to learn for me too when it comes to engine swaps, but i think you'll be able to use your radiator unless you're doing a tranny conversion too (stick to auto, i don know about auto to stick).||||reg||1
Z||000002||862M4inCA||03-21-2001||10:59 AM||||Right now I have the Isuzu 5 sp. in mine, and after reading the page that explained the conversion the guy who did the conversion also used the isuzu 5 sp and has had no problems with it in the year and a half since he wrote the page. He does drive a little harder than I would since I don't plan to autocross like he does so I'm sure the tranny wouldn't have that much extra stress. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jason Francisco||03-21-2001||01:20 PM||||I'm doing the switch now.  My friend, a mechanic, is doing it for me.  He nows Fieros well as he has one and always has had one (it's his engine I'm putting in).  He says it's no probblem to switch the engines.  I know he's changing the ECM, fuel pump, fly wheel and changing my clutch, because mines going.  The engine I'm putting in is an auto but I'm keeping my 5 speed.  I have also heard, like you, that the 5 speed is good (strong) enough.<P>------------------<BR>'86 2m4/modified<BR>Pixies rule!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jason Francisco||03-21-2001||01:21 PM||||Also, while it's on the floor, we're changing the timing belt, water pump and oil pump.  I figure, why not spend a few extra bucks and change some criticle engine components.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Voytek||03-21-2001||02:00 PM||||862M4: You shouldn't have any problems.  I was going to do this at one point but ended up buying a fast back instead.  As far as I know, you don't need to change the radiator or the wiring for the dash.  Just unplug the old computer and plug the new one in.  Fuel pump is one thing you need to change.  Other than that, probably a few minor wiring adjustments here and there and you're set.  Mechanically it should all fit no problem.||||reg||1
Z||000006||862M4inCA||03-21-2001||02:01 PM||||you bring up a good point about the flywheel. Would I need to put the 2.5 flywheel on the 2.8? or will the clutch bolt up to the existing 2.8? Also, it wouldn't be a bad idea to go ahead and change the timing set. I've had bad experiences with the 2.5 timing setup, and wouldn't want to relive that, even though the 2.8 is said to be easier. The guy didn't mention if it came from an auto or manual, but I'm hoping it's from a manual. I'm trying to get into contact with him sometime soon to get more details about the engine. So far I know it's a V6 with 113K on it. He said the engine ran great but noticed after a week of sitting it blew a puff of greyish smoke on startup (which really doesn't worry me since mine does it everytime I start, not just after letting it sit a week). He's only asking $150 for the engine and all the parts he doesn't plan to use with the V8. Seems like a good enough deal to me.||||reg||1
